\chapter{Tutorial: Your first search}
\label{ch:tutorial-first-search}

[[first-search]] is the lesson a newcomer meets first.
Its object of learning is that a search in Scholar is a \emph{record}, not
a screenful of hits: the same words sent to two databases are two different
searches, the same search printed in two formats is one search, and a
session is such a record kept for later.
A learner who has understood this reads every later lesson correctly,
because sessions, decisions and exports all build on the recorded search.

The arc separates the three parts of the record one at a time, then fuses
them:
\begin{enumerate}
\item find out what databases there are (step~1), and run one query
  against one of them (step~2);
\item hold the query fixed and vary the database (step~3), then hold the
  hits fixed and vary the output format (step~4), so the learner
  experiences which of the two changes the result and which does not;
\item see why the database matters, through the syntax table (step~5);
\item keep the search as a session (step~6) and look at what was kept
  (step~7);
\item say, in one answer, what Scholar records (step~8).
\end{enumerate}
Steps~3 and~4 are the heart of the lesson: they are variation theory's
contrast pattern applied to the two aspects a learner most often confuses.
Both keep the query invariant, and each varies exactly one thing---the
provider or the format---so the learner can attribute the difference they
see to that one thing.
The order (provider first, format second) is deliberate: the provider
contrast produces a visibly different table, which is a strong experience,
and the format contrast then shows that a weaker-looking change (the same
papers, printed differently) is not a different search at all.

Every search in the lesson uses the query \enquote{literate programming}
and a limit of five.
The query is a stable, well-indexed topic whose top hit on OpenAlex is
Knuth's 1984 paper, so the learner sees a recognisable result, and the
small limit keeps each command fast.
The query text is never repeated in a required pattern with a title,
because rankings change; the patterns anchor on the command the learner
typed and on Scholar's own fixed output strings.
Only keyless providers appear, so the lesson runs on a fresh install.

\section{Tutorial source}

The whole tutorial is one tangled root chunk.
Each step lives in its own named chunk so the prose between them can
describe that step's role without breaking the Markdown that pytorial
reads.

<<[[first-search.md]]>>=
---
id: first-search
title: Your first search
summary: Run a search, see what varies between databases and formats, and keep it as a session.
---

<<providers step>>

<<first search step>>

<<other provider step>>

<<output format step>>

<<syntax step>>

<<named session step>>

<<sessions list step>>

<<what is recorded step>>
@

\section{Step 1: See which databases you can search}

The lesson opens with [[scholar providers list]] because it is the only
command that needs nothing: no query, no key, no session.
Its table is also the map for everything that follows---the learner needs
the provider names [[openalex]] and [[arxiv]] to type the next steps, and
the [[Configured]] column is where they first see that some databases
need a key and some do not.
The required patterns ask for the two providers the lesson will use, so
the check doubles as a sanity check that the install can see them.

<<providers step>>=
# See which databases you can search

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+providers\s+list\b
  - Available Search Providers
  - openalex
  - arxiv
hint: "Ask the providers group to list what is available."
```

Run `scholar providers list`.

Scholar searches several bibliographic databases, called *providers*.
The table shows each provider's name, whether it is configured, and
whether it needs an API key. Some providers work without any key; this
tutorial only uses those, so it works on a fresh install.

Note the names `openalex` and `arxiv`. You will use them next.
@

\section{Step 2: Search one database}

The first search is scoped to one provider and five results.
Scoping is not a simplification for the learner's sake alone: the default
(every configured provider) would produce a long merged table in which the
role of the provider is invisible, and the next step needs a table that
came from exactly one database so that changing the database changes the
table.
The [[-l 5]] limit keeps the whole lesson quick and makes the table fit on
one screen.
The patterns anchor on the command with its provider flag and on the table
title Scholar prints, [[Results for:]], which does not depend on which
papers come back.

<<first search step>>=
# Search one database

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+search\s+.*-p\s+openalex\b
  - "Results for: literate programming"
hint: "Quote the query, pick the provider with -p, and cap the hits with -l."
```

Run:

```
scholar search "literate programming" -p openalex -l 5
```

`-p openalex` sends the query to one provider only, and `-l 5` asks for
at most five results from it. Without `-p`, Scholar would query every
provider it can reach and merge the results.

Look at the table: title, authors, year, DOI. The first hit should be
Knuth's 1984 paper that coined the term.
@

\section{Step 3: Same query, another database}

This is the first contrast.
Everything stays as in step~2 except the provider, and the table changes
completely: arXiv indexes preprints, so \enquote{literate programming}
finds different papers there, some only loosely related.
The learner experiences that a provider is a lens, not a neutral
pipe---the thing they need to know before they ever trust a
\enquote{not found}.
The prose asks them to compare, but does not tell them what they will
see; the pattern only requires the [[arxiv]] flag and the table title.

<<other provider step>>=
# Same query, another database

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+search\s+.*-p\s+arxiv\b
  - "Results for: literate programming"
hint: "Change only the provider name after -p; keep the query and the limit."
```

Run the same search against arXiv:

```
scholar search "literate programming" -p arxiv -l 5
```

Compare the two tables. The query is identical, but the papers are not:
each provider indexes different literature and ranks it differently.
This is why a serious search asks several providers, and why a paper that
one provider does not return may still exist.
@

\section{Step 4: Same search, another format}

The second contrast varies the output format while holding everything
else fixed, including the provider.
BibTeX first, because a bibliography entry is the form the learner most
likely wants eventually and because [[@article{]] is unmistakable; then
JSON, piped through [[head]], to show the same search as data.
Seeing the identical DOI in the table, the BibTeX entry and the JSON
record is what makes the point: the format changes how the record is
printed, not what was searched.
The prose also explains the [[auto]] format, because a learner who later
redirects output to a file will otherwise be surprised to get CSV.
Both required patterns anchor on the format flag and one fixed token of
each format.

<<output format step>>=
# Same search, another format

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+search\s+.*-f\s+bibtex\b
  - "@article{"
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+search\s+.*-f\s+json\b
  - '"papers"'
hint: "Run the openalex search twice more, once with -f bibtex and once with -f json piped into head."
```

Run the OpenAlex search again, twice, changing only the output format:

```
scholar search "literate programming" -p openalex -l 5 -f bibtex
scholar search "literate programming" -p openalex -l 5 -f json | head -20
```

The papers are the same as in step 2; only the presentation differs.
BibTeX gives you entries to paste into a bibliography. JSON gives you the
full record, including the query, provider, timestamp and limit that
produced it.

The default format is `auto`: a table when the output goes to a
terminal, CSV when it goes to a file or a pipe. Pick a format explicitly
whenever you script Scholar.
@

\section{Step 5: Why the database matters}

Step~3 showed \emph{that} providers differ; this step shows one reason
\emph{why}.
[[scholar syntax]] prints, per provider, which boolean operators, phrase
search and wildcards are understood.
Placing it after the contrast rather than before it follows the
try-first-tell-later order: the learner has already seen two providers
disagree, so the table answers a question they now have.
The lesson does not ask them to use any operator---that would introduce a
third varying aspect---but the prose points at the row for a provider
that treats [[AND]] as a plain word, which is the most common trap.

<<syntax step>>=
# Why the database matters

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+syntax\b
  - Query Syntax Reference
hint: "There is a command that prints the query syntax each provider understands."
```

Run `scholar syntax`.

Each provider has its own query language. The reference shows which of
them understand `AND`, `OR`, `NOT`, grouping, quoted phrases and
wildcards. Some treat operators as ordinary words, and one strips them
out entirely.

So the same words can mean a different query on different providers,
which is one more reason the tables in steps 2 and 3 differ.
@

\section{Step 6: Keep the search as a session}

Only now, with the record understood, does the learner keep one.
The step repeats the step~2 command with a single addition, [[-n]],
so the difference between \enquote{search} and \enquote{search and keep}
is exactly one flag.
The confirmation line goes to standard error, which the prose mentions
because the previous step just taught that standard output can be
redirected; a learner who redirects this command still sees the
confirmation.
The required pattern anchors on the flag and on [[Saved session:]] alone,
not on the count that follows it, because Rich may wrap the long path
that separates the two.

<<named session step>>=
# Keep the search as a session

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+search\s+.*-n\s+my-first-review\b
  - "Saved session:"
hint: "Repeat the openalex search and name the session with -n."
```

Run the OpenAlex search once more, this time giving it a name:

```
scholar search "literate programming" -p openalex -l 5 -n my-first-review
```

`-n my-first-review` saves the search as a *review session*: the query,
the provider, the limit, the time, and every paper found, each waiting
for a keep-or-discard decision. The confirmation is printed on standard
error, so it still shows when you redirect the results to a file.

Searching again with the same `-n` name appends to the session instead
of replacing it. That is how one review collects several searches.
@

\section{Step 7: Look at what was kept}

Two commands, one step: [[sessions list]] shows that the session exists,
[[sessions show]] shows what is inside it.
Because the tutorial shell keeps Scholar's data directory inside the
tutorial's own workspace, the list contains exactly the session the
learner just made---the lesson can promise this, and a learner who has
used Scholar before is not confused by their real sessions.
The [[show]] output opens the recorded queries with the line
[[Queries by provider:]] and lists every paper as pending; the pattern
requires both, which confirms that the record holds the query and the
hits, the two things step~8 will ask about.
In a terminal [[show]] uses a pager, so the prose tells the learner how
to leave it.

<<sessions list step>>=
# Look at what was kept

```tutorial-step
required_patterns:
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+list\b
  - my-first-review
  - mode: regex
    pattern: (?:^|\s)scholar\s+sessions\s+show\s+my-first-review\b
  - "Queries by provider:"
  - "Pending (5)"
hint: "The sessions group has a list verb and a show verb; give show the session name."
```

Run:

```
scholar sessions list
scholar sessions show my-first-review
```

The list holds exactly one session, because this tutorial keeps its own
data directory; your real sessions are untouched.

`show` prints the recorded queries, provider by provider, and then every
paper with its status. All five are *pending*: found, but not yet judged.
If the output opens in a pager, press `q` to leave it.
@

\section{Step 8: What does Scholar record?}

The closing question is the fusion move: after separating provider,
format and session, the learner names the record as a whole.
The options are built so that each wrong answer corresponds to one of the
lesson's contrasts.
\enquote{Only the query} is refuted by step~3, where the same query gave
two different results; \enquote{only the papers} is refuted by step~7,
where [[show]] printed the queries before the papers.
The correct option lists the parts the learner saw in the JSON of step~4
and the [[show]] output of step~7.

<<what is recorded step>>=
# What does Scholar record?

```tutorial-step
kind: single_select
options:
  - Only the query text
  - The query, the provider, the limit, the time and the papers found
  - Only the papers found
answers:
  - The query, the provider, the limit, the time and the papers found
hint: "Think of what `sessions show` printed before the paper list, and of the JSON in step 4."
```

Which of these does a saved search record, so that it can be repeated
later?

Pick the answer that matches what you saw in `sessions show` and in the
JSON output.
@
